# Executed by build.py with its page-authoring helpers in scope.
new_context='''Original requirement context: MongoDB was selected for federation/replication, not the document model. The worked case is Singapore (SG) authoritative home, UK operational projection, US excluded. The bank is evaluating a self-managed on-premises platform; separate database infrastructure management from customer federation. Eventual consistency is the working prototype choice, not yet a confirmed business SLA. A five-second lag and 30-second remote-read lease are provisional test settings. Do not silently choose indefinite offline reads or automatic writer promotion. Evaluate logical data replication (LDR) as a third transport option without assuming dynamic row filtering or OPA integration. Preserve local HA and current customer generation fences regardless of transport.\n\n'''
for p in pages:
    p['prompt']=new_context+p['prompt']
baseprompt=new_context+baseprompt
by={p['slug']:p for p in pages}
by['index']['body']=call('Edition 03 · original discussion incorporated','Singapore is the authoritative home, the UK holds the permitted operational copy, and the US is excluded. On-premises estate management and customer-data federation are separate layers. Consistency, outage reads and LDR suitability are now explicit decision tracks—not implied agreements.')+by['index']['body']
by['index']['body']+=section('Trace the reasoning', '''<div class="link-grid"><a href="project-context.html"><span>ORIGINAL REQUIREMENTS</span><h3>From MongoDB to federation →</h3><p>What you asked, what was proposed, and how the topology evolved.</p></a><a href="platform.html"><span>TWO CONTROL PLANES</span><h3>Operate the platform on premises →</h3><p>Regional Kubernetes, database operators and customer policy controllers.</p></a><a href="decisions.html"><span>OPEN DESIGN DECISIONS</span><h3>Explore the outage trade-off →</h3><p>Strict revocation bounds versus explicitly authorised offline reads.</p></a></div>''')
by['architecture']['body']=section('Two management layers, separate responsibilities',table(['Layer','Owns','Does not own'],[
['Infrastructure control plane','Regional Kubernetes, database lifecycle, capacity, certificates and restore procedures','Per-customer entitlement and field-distribution decisions.'],
['Customer federation control plane','OPA policy, desired/observed copies, bootstrap, revoke and evidence','Database quorum, node repair or Kubernetes lifecycle.']]))+'<p>Use three independent regional database clusters for the working selective-replication design. A single logical cluster across three Kubernetes clusters was the earlier general infrastructure suggestion; the later per-customer exclusion requirement changes that topology decision. <a href="platform.html">Platform design</a> · <a href="replication-options.html">Three replication options</a>.</p>'+by['architecture']['body']
by['architecture']['body']=by['architecture']['body'].replace('Expired remote read leases fail closed.','Under the default test profile, expired remote read leases fail closed; an alternative bounded offline-read profile needs prior approval.').replace('Customer policy authority lives with the home record.','Customer policy authority lives with the home record in Singapore for C-001.')
by['gateways']['body']+=section('When Singapore is unreachable','<p>UK can serve its existing permitted projection only while its applicable read authorisation remains valid and the data meets the chosen freshness limit. In the strict test profile, expiry blocks further reads even though the UK database is healthy. A separately pre-authorised offline-read profile can allow a bounded interval for selected low-risk operations. It never permits home-authoritative writes. A UK client requiring current Singapore state receives an unavailable response when routing cannot complete.</p><p><a href="decisions.html">Compare outage-read profiles and their revocation bounds →</a></p>')
by['opa']['body']+=section('OPA and offline-read permissions','<p>The federation rule on this page continues to deny expired facts. Do not bypass that check to implement availability. If the bank chooses an offline-read mode, issue a separate signed read grant in advance, scoped to UK, service, purpose, dataset, fields, generation, operation and an absolute expiry. A distinct <code>bank.access.offline_read</code> rule evaluates that grant, verified local identity, clock bounds, data freshness and local fences. No writes, new exports or grant renewals are authorised by that offline permission.</p><p>Seeing a newer local denial or fence always wins. A revocation not yet delivered during a partition cannot be discovered by local OPA. This is the residual window to approve and test. <a href="decisions.html">Decision D-03</a>.</p>')
by['transactions']['body']=call('Consistency decision remains open','Local SERIALIZABLE transactions are the chosen prototype boundary. Cross-region eventual consistency is provisional: the earlier discussion asked whether UK must see updates before Singapore commits, but no answer was recorded. Five-second replication lag is a benchmark target only.')+by['transactions']['body']
by['transactions']['body']+=section('If synchronous cross-region visibility becomes mandatory','<p>Revisit the native distributed-database option and permitted quorum topology before implementation. Waiting for a destination acknowledgement after a home commit changes response timing but does not make two independent clusters one atomic transaction. A timeout can leave the home committed and the caller uncertain. Measure the WAN coordination cost and define exactly whether the requirement concerns commit atomicity, response visibility or read-your-write behaviour.</p>')
by['federation']['body']+=section('Alternative transport: logical data replication','<p>Keep the custom outbox/CDC exporter as the baseline PoC and run a bounded LDR investigation. LDR might transport a dedicated table containing only UK-authorised rows/fields, but approval, projection maintenance, revocation fencing, access checks and evidence would remain custom. Do not connect the full source customer table to UK and filter after arrival. <a href="replication-options.html">Read the option comparison and evaluation gates →</a>.</p>')
by['lifecycle']['body']+=call('An outage-read grant changes access-blocking semantics','With a pre-authorised offline read grant, a disconnected destination can continue specified reads until that grant expires unless it receives a newer local deny. Source export blocking can complete earlier. Show both timestamps; do not use the 30-second strict test lease as a universal guarantee. The UX simulator on the decisions page illustrates the difference.')
by['operations']['body']=call('Which outage is being simulated?','This screen models the UK destination becoming unreachable during revocation. The separate <a href="decisions.html">outage-read simulator</a> models Singapore authority becoming unreachable while UK remains healthy. These are different failures; neither implies completed deletion.')+by['operations']['body']
by['control-plane']['body']+=section('Show operational authority alongside location','<p>The Singapore workspace below owns C-001. The UK view would show a projection plus read-authorisation mode, grant expiry, data age and current known fence. A physically present copy does not itself confer permission to read. Add a conspicuous “home authority unavailable” state with the specific deadline for remaining permitted reads.</p><p><a href="decisions.html">Explore the read-authorisation wireframe →</a></p>')
by['engineering']['body']=section('Before the implementation slices',table(['Track','Required output'],[
['Self-hosted platform','Validate bank on-premises region/zone mapping, selected operator release, Kubernetes support, licensing and management-outage behaviour.'],
['Consistency decision','Compare async projections against required UK visibility. Keep five-second lag explicitly provisional.'],
['Outage reads','Test strict short lease and bounded pre-authorised read-only profiles; decide operation scope, lifetime, skew and freshness.'],
['LDR spike','Test pre-approved destination projections, revocation races, schema and DLQ behaviour before substituting the transport.']]))+by['engineering']['body']
by['engineering']['body']+=section('Additional acceptance evidence','<ol class="numbered"><li>Disconnect central GitOps/fleet management while keeping regional clusters up: local services and database recovery remain operable.</li><li>Disconnect Singapore authority with UK alive: demonstrate strict lease expiry, then a separate explicitly pre-authorised offline-read test. Neither permits authoritative UK writes.</li><li>Run identical source data through baseline export and the optional LDR spike. Inspect forbidden rows, fields, histories and DLQs at UK before declaring equivalence.</li><li>Record every unresolved decision in an ADR with owner, evidence required and provisional configuration; do not turn defaults into requirements.</li></ol>')
by['engineering']['prompt']+='\nBefore coding, read Origin & requirements, On-prem platform control, Replication options and Consistency & outage reads. Implement the Singapore-home worked case throughout. Report the async-vs-sync and strict-vs-offline experiments as alternatives, not approved bank decisions. Treat Continuum adoption as a separate procurement/readiness assessment; do not depend on unreleased self-hosted management features.'
by['references']['body']+=section('New source checks for edition 03',sources([
('Continuum architecture and RoachMgr availability statement','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/blog/continuum-architecture/'),
('New Kubernetes Operator announcement, 5 August 2026','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/blog/cockroachdb-kubernetes-operator/'),
('CockroachDB logical data replication overview','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/logical-data-replication-overview'),
('Setting up unidirectional or bidirectional LDR','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/set-up-logical-data-replication')]))+call('Transcript and evidence handling','Both uploaded transcript parts were read in full. They establish the original intent and the progression of recommendations. Earlier vendor-feature claims are not automatically treated as current fact; this edition checks the specific operator, RoachMgr and LDR claims used in the design. Product availability still requires a pinned-version and support review before deployment.')
by['references']['body']=by['references']['body'].replace('15 real HTML pages','19 real HTML pages').replace('a published static design site','a published static design site, edition 03, with 19 pages').replace('Map to exact approved cloud regions, AZs and providers.','Map to approved on-premises sites and local failure domains first; explicitly approve any cloud or hybrid extension.')

add('project-context','The original intent, carried through.','Context / Transcript reconciliation','The starting question was whether CockroachDB and Continuum could improve a MongoDB estate selected for geographic federation and replication.',
section('What the original discussion established',table(['Context','Design implication','Status'],[
['MongoDB chosen for federation/replication, not documents','Evaluate relational and transaction gains while preserving distribution and resilience.','Confirmed context'],
['Self-managed on-premises control-plane question','Baseline the platform on infrastructure operated by the bank.','Evaluation direction'],
['One global customer ID; Singapore authoritative; UK operational copy; US excluded','Use the same identifier only in permitted contexts; no universal customer directory required.','Worked scenario'],
['Single point of master acceptable','Keep one writer epoch; local home transaction is the authority.','Confirmed'],
['Daemon responding to permissions and entitlements','Policy authority, reconciliation, selective data movement and continuous assurance.','Confirmed'],
['Local HA in every region','Three local failure domains where available; WAN federation is a separate concern.','Confirmed'],
['Strong cross-region consistency or a few seconds of delay?','Run comparison; do not present async delay as agreed.','Unanswered'],
['Thin prototype, diagrams, APIs and extension points','No full banking product or production deployment.','Confirmed'],
['OPA, OpenTelemetry, gateways and UX','Subsequent additions in this design conversation.','Confirmed']]))+
section('How the topology recommendation evolved',flow([
['General platform evaluation','A single logical CockroachDB cluster across three regional Kubernetes clusters was initially suggested for distributed SQL and operations.'],
['Customer-specific exclusion','The requirement then narrowed to SG + UK only, local resilience, selective fields and changing entitlements.'],
['Working federation design','Independent regional clusters plus controlled asynchronous distribution became the preferred prototype, conditional on consistency requirements.']]))+
section('Keep three evaluations separate',table(['Evaluation','Question','Evidence'],[
['Database engine','Can CockroachDB support the workload, SQL model and transaction invariants?','Compatibility, serializable retries, load, failures and restore tests.'],
['Estate platform / Continuum','Does pooling and management improve operational effort, isolation and cost?','Supported deployment model, resource isolation, control-plane failure tests, commercial terms.'],
['Customer federation','Can policies prevent forbidden transfers and manage grants/revokes correctly?','Negative residency tests, race tests, regional evidence and retention inventory.']]))+
section('Migration and transactional consolidation','<p>Investigate three shapes: preserve isolated service databases; bring transactionally related domains into a shared home database and process; or use a hybrid. The hybrid is the working preference: consolidate only domains with genuine atomicity and performance benefit, retaining separate ownership elsewhere. Separate repositories can produce versioned in-process modules; repository boundaries need not become network transaction boundaries.</p><p>Do not assume independent virtual clusters share ACID transactions. Define the actual cluster, SQL session and permission boundary. Benchmark the same business operation before and after consolidation, including network handoffs, retries, contention and operational complexity—not throughput alone.</p>')+
section('What changed in edition 03',table(['Earlier site','Integrated design'],[
['UK-home illustrative customer','SG home → UK projection throughout samples, wireframes and prompts.'],
['Implicit regional platform','Dedicated on-premises infrastructure control-plane design and support gates.'],
['Custom federation only','Native placement, custom federation and LDR comparison.'],
['Five-second lag and 30-second lease defaults','Clearly provisional settings with named decisions and outage simulation.'],
['Short-lease reads imply availability limitation','Explicit strict versus pre-authorised offline-read choice; no automatic promotion.']]))+
call('Transcript reconciliation, not blind adoption','The earlier illustrative SQL is not copied as an executable placement recipe. Any native-placement branch must use the selected CockroachDB version’s supported configuration and verify every replica and operational copy. Likewise, platform maturity claims require product-specific evidence.'),
baseprompt+'Create a requirements traceability matrix from the original MongoDB/Continuum context and later federation brief. Preserve confirmed constraints and identify unanswered consistency/outage questions. Compare isolated databases, a common home transaction boundary and a hybrid. Produce an ADR explaining why a single global cluster was an earlier option and three regional clusters are the current conditional preference. Do not implement full product code.',group='Explore')

add('platform','Two control planes, operated on premises.','Component / Platform & database operations','Use a regional infrastructure layer to run the databases and a separate customer-policy layer to govern their contents.',
section('Responsibility boundaries', '''<div class="architecture-grid"><div class="cell"><span class="eyebrow">FLEET GOVERNANCE</span><h3>Approved change</h3><div class="cell-layer">GitOps configuration and change approval</div><div class="cell-layer">Kubernetes fleet platform</div><div class="cell-layer">Aggregate infrastructure health</div></div><div class="cell"><span class="eyebrow">REGIONAL OPERATIONS</span><h3>Run the database</h3><div class="cell-layer">Local Kubernetes management</div><div class="cell-layer">CockroachDB Operator</div><div class="cell-layer">Three local failure domains</div></div><div class="cell"><span class="eyebrow">CUSTOMER FEDERATION</span><h3>Govern the data</h3><div class="cell-layer">OPA and entitlement authority</div><div class="cell-layer">Exporter / importer / reconciler</div><div class="cell-layer">Regional evidence and retention</div></div></div><p>The database continues serving when central fleet/GitOps management is unreachable. Regional operators retain break-glass procedures with audit and later reconciliation. Customer-serving paths must not synchronously depend on a central Kubernetes manager.</p>''')+
section('Baseline deployment',table(['Scope','Proposed arrangement'],[
['Sites','SG, UK and US each run an independent Kubernetes cluster and an independent CockroachDB cluster.'],
['Local HA','At least three database nodes across verified local power/rack/zone failure domains, quorum-safe placement and spare capacity. Three racks do not prove full regional disaster survival.'],
['Operator','Evaluate the current CockroachDB Kubernetes Operator for the pinned database/Kubernetes versions; identify it explicitly rather than adopting the legacy public operator by name alone.'],
['Fleet platform','Use the bank’s established platform: OpenShift where already standard, or evaluate Rancher-managed Kubernetes. These are alternatives, not mandatory combined dependencies.'],
['GitOps','Separate regional applications/overlays and credentials; no customer-level policies or secrets in a broadly replicated infrastructure repository.'],
['Regional services','Private registry/mirrors, PKI/KMS integration, backup vault, DNS, Kafka, OTel and audit services need a supported on-premises mapping.'],
['Change management','Drain and upgrade one failure domain at a time after health gates; test certificate rotation, restore and rollback constraints.']]))+
section('Continuum and RoachMgr: a separate adoption decision','<p>Cockroach Labs describes Continuum as estate infrastructure built on CockroachDB, including virtual clusters. Its architecture article describes RoachMgr as powering cloud deployments, with public self-hosted availability still future in that published statement. A planned release is not evidence of a supported installation available to the bank. Do not make it a dependency of this on-premises PoC.</p>'+sources([('Vendor architecture and RoachMgr statement','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/blog/continuum-architecture/')]))+
section('Operator evidence and limits','<p>The 5 August 2026 vendor announcement states that the new Kubernetes Operator is generally available for self-hosted workloads and describes GitOps, private-registry and air-gapped support. This is vendor capability evidence, not certification of the bank’s specific OpenShift/Rancher versions, storage drivers or three-site topology. Obtain the support matrix and run failure/upgrade tests.</p>'+sources([('Vendor Kubernetes Operator announcement','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/blog/cockroachdb-kubernetes-operator/')]))+
section('Infrastructure inventory skeleton',code('''# Bank-owned design input, not a CockroachDB Operator CRD.
platform:
  deployment: self_managed_on_premises
  fleet_manager: existing_bank_standard  # OpenShift OR Rancher evaluation
  central_manager_required_for_serving: false
regions:
  SG: {database_cluster: customer-sg, local_failure_domains: [a, b, c]}
  UK: {database_cluster: customer-uk, local_failure_domains: [a, b, c]}
  US: {database_cluster: customer-us, local_failure_domains: [a, b, c]}
links:
  database_raft_between_regions: disabled
  cross_region_sql: denied
  federation: explicitly_authorised_only
  global_customer_directory: disabled
validation:
  operator_version: pin_and_verify
  kubernetes_support: required
  storage_and_restore: required''','YAML'))+
section('Enterprise readiness gates',table(['Gate','Required evidence'],[
['Support and lifecycle','Pinned versions, licences, upgrade path, operator compatibility and contractual escalation.'],
['Failure isolation','Central manager outage, local AZ/node loss, WAN partition, local PKI/DNS outages.'],
['Isolation / cost','For any future virtualisation, test noisy neighbours, quotas, shared failure domains, replication/egress and support costs.'],
['Operational sovereignty','Inspect remote support, telemetry/AI connectors, backup locations and administrator processing locations.'],
['Recovery','Restore with current policy/fences before opening serving traffic; separately decide whole-region RPO/RTO.']]))+
call('Independent judgement','CockroachDB engine suitability, new platform features and this custom federation layer have different evidence bases. No claim of bank production readiness follows from a vendor GA label alone. Aegis, Plenum and autoscaling remain optional evaluation topics; verify their exact edition/deployment status if they become requirements.'),
baseprompt+'Design the self-managed on-premises platform layer using the bank’s existing Kubernetes standard and a supported CockroachDB Operator release. Use three independent regional database identities, not one Raft cluster spread across sites. Provide GitOps overlays, credentials/network boundaries and runbooks. Validate support for selected OpenShift or Rancher versions, storage and air-gap needs. Prove customer-serving continuity during central management outage. Do not depend on future RoachMgr availability or grant platform operators implicit customer-data access.')

add('replication-options','Three ways to move data. One policy obligation.','Decision / Replication alternatives','Compare database placement, custom selective federation and logical data replication against the same SG → UK, never-US scenario.',
section('Option comparison',table(['Option','Consistency / scope','Benefit','Main constraint'],[
['A · Native single-cluster placement','Distributed transactions inside one CockroachDB cluster; all replica placement validated','Database manages consistency and replication','Permitted quorum placement and WAN latency; locality alone is not customer policy.'],
['B · Outbox + local CDC + controlled exporter','Async domain snapshots between independent clusters','Explicit OPA checks, field projection, permits, replay fences and receipts','Custom transport/reconciliation code; eventual consistency must be acceptable.'],
['C · CockroachDB LDR','Native logical table replication between self-hosted clusters','May reduce transport implementation for approved tables','Table scope does not establish dynamic per-customer/field policy or purge semantics.']]))+
section('What current LDR documentation establishes','<p>The current overview describes self-hosted support, table-level initial copying and ongoing eventual replication, unidirectional/bidirectional arrangements, conflict handling and DLQs. It does not establish an OPA authorisation hook for each customer transfer in this design. Pin the release and verify its supported schemas and topology before prototyping.</p>'+sources([('LDR overview','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/logical-data-replication-overview'),('LDR setup','https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/set-up-logical-data-replication')]))+
section('A constrained LDR experiment',flow([
['Project in Singapore','OPA-authorised projector materialises only UK-permitted customer fields in a dedicated source table. No full-PII source stream to UK.'],
['Replicate one way','Evaluate LDR from that approved projection table to a UK staging table. Keep destination application writes disabled.'],
['Fence before serving','An importer/view enforcement layer checks generation and grants before promoting or exposing data. Staging and DLQ remain governed UK copies.'],
['Revoke and inspect','Delete source projection, install UK fence, and account for in-flight stream/history before declaring effective completion. Test delayed row resurrection.']]))+
call('Staging is already residency','Filtering at the destination can stop access, but cannot undo data crossing the border. This experiment is acceptable only while UK is a permitted receiving region. Excluding UK later requires stopping/fencing the stream and proving its in-flight and retained-copy lifecycle; it cannot rely solely on a served-view filter.')+
section('Evaluation matrix before adopting LDR',table(['Question','Pass condition'],[
['Selective content','All initial and incremental source rows/fields are approved before transfer; no forbidden metadata in source keys.'],
['Entitlement updates','Grant, expiry and field narrowing trigger materialised projection changes without business writes.'],
['Revocation','Paused/restarted jobs, delayed bootstrap and stale rows cannot re-enable access or produce falsely complete purge evidence.'],
['Order / schema / deletes','Actual selected-version behaviour is measured; do not assume multi-row transaction atomicity at the destination.'],
['DLQ and retention','Inspect failed-row payloads, historical copies and restores; stopping a stream is not deletion.'],
['Operational fit','Network openings, credentials, per-destination tables/jobs, HA, cost and throughput compare favourably with baseline.']]))+
section('Recommendation and switching rule','<p>Keep option B as the thin PoC baseline because the policy-to-transfer relationship is explicit. Evaluate C behind a transport adapter; retain it only if it proves the same lifecycle and custody properties with less operational burden. Revisit A if synchronous transactional visibility is mandatory. Native replication stays responsible for local HA in either B or C.</p><p>Do not use LDR’s ability to accept writes in both clusters as permission to make this customer multi-master. Single-home authority remains a business choice until a separate conflict and promotion design is approved.</p>'),
baseprompt+'Run a bounded LDR spike alongside the baseline federation prototype. Use self-hosted clusters and a dedicated UK-approved source projection in SG. Verify exact version limitations and never stream the raw customer master to an excluded destination. Compare bootstrap, field narrowing, deletion, lag, restart, DLQ, generation fences and restore behaviour. Record whether destination staging/history contains customer data after revocation. Keep authority single-home and produce an ADR recommending adopt/reject/defer based on evidence.')

add('decisions','Make consistency and outage behaviour explicit.','Decision / Availability & revocation','The original conversation left replication delay unanswered. This design exposes the choice instead of turning demonstration settings into a bank requirement.',
section('Decision register',table(['ID','Decision','Current working position','Status'],[
['D-01','Cross-region commit visibility','Async projections for PoC; benchmark p95 ≤5 s under specified load','Business acceptance open'],
['D-02','Home authority','SG home / UK copy / US excluded for C-001; one writer epoch','Worked example confirmed'],
['D-03','UK reads during SG outage','Strict 30-second read lease is the default test profile; compare bounded offline grant','Policy approval open'],
['D-04','Write failover','No UK authoritative writes or automatic promotion during SG outage','PoC boundary; recovery design open'],
['D-05','Transport','Outbox/CDC baseline; bounded LDR spike','Candidate assessment open'],
['D-06','Platform','Self-managed on-premises; operator + existing bank Kubernetes standard','Support/topology validation open']]))+
section('Read availability is not copy availability',table(['Mode','UK behaviour when SG cannot be reached','Revocation trade-off'],[
['Strict short lease','Serve only until signed read lease expiry; then deny','Short bounded stale-authority window; low independent availability.'],
['Pre-authorised offline read','Serve specified read-only purposes/fields until a separate absolute deadline, subject to data age and local deny','Longer explicitly accepted exposure to unseen remote revocation.'],
['Indefinite offline access','Not a supported prototype mode','No finite guarantee that a remote revocation takes effect.']]))+
call('Engineering judgement','Keep the strict mode as the safety-first default in the PoC, but implement the alternative as a separate test profile so the bank can choose knowingly. Do not renew the offline grant during the partition, allow exports with expired federation facts, or bypass a known local revocation.')+
'''<div class="demo-banner"><span class="status-dot"></span> OUTAGE READ SIMULATOR · UK remains healthy; Singapore authority is unreachable</div><div class="lab-grid"><div class="panel"><h2>Read authorisation profile</h2><label>Mode<select id="outage-mode"><option value="strict">Strict — 30-second lease</option><option value="offline">Pre-authorised offline read — 120 seconds</option></select></label><label>Seconds since last authorisation<input id="outage-elapsed" type="range" min="0" max="180" value="45" step="1"><span id="outage-elapsed-label">45 seconds</span></label><label class="check"><input id="outage-local-deny" type="checkbox"> UK has received a local revoke fence</label><label class="check"><input id="outage-stale" type="checkbox"> Projection exceeds approved data-age limit</label><p class="muted">Illustrative grants issued just before the outage. Expiry blocks at the stated deadline; clock error can shift real-world observation by the assumed 2-second bound. Neither lifetime has bank approval.</p></div><div class="panel decision-panel"><span class="eyebrow">UK READ OUTCOME</span><div id="outage-result" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div>'''+
section('Offline-read contract skeleton',code('''# Separate from export authorisation; issued before disconnection.
offline_read_grant:
  customer: synthetic-customer-001
  home_region: SG
  serving_region: UK
  service: customer-servicing
  purpose: account-support
  operations: [read]
  fields: [customerId, displayName]
  grant_generation: 3
  issued_at: signed_server_time
  valid_until: absolute_signed_deadline
  maximum_data_age_seconds: bank_decision_required
  can_renew_offline: false
# Require verified signature, matching identity/purpose/region, unexpired grant,
# valid local user identity, acceptable data age and no newer local deny/fence.
# Expired federation facts STILL deny exports; this is a different read grant.''','YAML'))+
section('Synchronous visibility needs precise language','<p>“UK sees the change before Singapore commits” is stronger than “the API responds after UK has applied it”. The latter can be implemented as a post-commit wait but is not an atomic cross-cluster transaction: Singapore may commit and UK may fail before acknowledgement. If atomic global visibility is required, revisit database-native placement/quorum or another explicitly designed coordination protocol; do not disguise a post-commit wait as ACID.</p>')+
section('Tests that resolve the choice','<p>Measure home transaction latency, healthy projection lag and UK read freshness. Partition SG from UK immediately after issuing a grant, revoke at SG, and measure when UK reads stop in each profile. Repeat with delivered local fences, expired user tokens, excessive data age and clock skew. Record RPO/RTO for recovery separately from authorisation lifetime and from replication lag.</p>'),
baseprompt+'Implement two explicitly separated outage-read test profiles: strict short lease and a pre-issued signed bounded offline read grant. Keep expired export facts fail-closed in both. Validate local identity, scope, region, purpose, fields, generation, data freshness, signature and absolute expiry. Known local fences override both profiles. Test unseen remote revoke under partition, clock skew and grant expiry. No offline renewal, authoritative UK writes or automatic promotion. Produce measurements and an ADR; do not claim the bank has approved the illustrative 30/120-second values.')

# Order navigation by the story, then components, then delivery.
order=['index','project-context','control-plane','policy-lab','operations','architecture','platform','gateways','opa','transactions','replication-options','federation','lifecycle','decisions','observability','audit','apis','engineering','references']
pages.sort(key=lambda p:order.index(p['slug']))
